WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
A C++ application may receive input through many channels.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:11.320
Standard input can be typed by a person, redirected from a file, or streamed from another process.

3
00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:17.980
Command-line arguments can be missing, reordered, very long, or encoded in unexpected ways.

4
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:24.240
Environment variables can influence paths, feature flags, credentials, or library behavior.

5
00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:32.580
Files and configuration files may be malformed, stale, partially written, or supplied by a user who does not know the expected format.

6
00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.560
Each source needs explicit assumptions.

7
00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:44.080
Network sockets, APIs, serialized data, binary protocols, device input, and library callbacks add more complexity.

8
00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.780
Sockets may deliver partial messages or multiple messages at once.

9
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:54.600
APIs may receive unexpected content types, missing fields, or values from older clients.

10
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:59.900
Serialized formats may carry nested structures, counts, and version fields.

11
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.920
Device input may fail, return data out of range, or report values with timing constraints.

12
00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:10.260
A callback may feel internal, but the data passed to it may still be untrusted.

13
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:16.620
A trust boundary is the point where data crosses from a less trusted context into code that may act on it.

14
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.040
Good input handling identifies those boundaries and applies checks close to them.

15
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:29.380
Define maximum sizes, expected encodings, allowed formats, ownership rules, and completion criteria before parsing deeply.

16
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:41.320
Decide whether input is text or binary, whether embedded null bytes are valid, whether missing fields are allowed, and which values can influence files, memory allocation, indexes, or control flow.

17
00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.940
Clear boundaries keep downstream code from relying on guesswork.
